L1: To investigate the effect of aerobic training in the context of antioxidant supplementation on systemic oxidative stress and leukocytes heat shock protein (Hsp)72 expression in the elderly. Sixteen septuagenarians (8 males and 8 females, mean age 74.6) were supplemented with Vitamin C and E (respectively 500 and 100mg per day) and randomly assigned either to sedentary (AS) or individualized aerobically trained (AT) group for 8 weeks. Plasma Vitamin C and E concentrations and aerobic fitness, as well as resting and post graded exercise (GXT) Hsp72 expression in leukocytes, plasma levels of thiobarbituric acid reactive substances (TBARS) and advanced oxidation protein product (AOPP) were measured pre and post training / supplementation. At the end of the intervention, the two groups showed a significant increase in resting plasma vitamin C and E (approximately 50 and 20% increase respectively) and a significant decrease in both resting and post GXT plasma TBARS and AOPP (approximately 25 and 20% decrease respectively). These changes were of similar magnitude in the two groups. The reduced oxidative stress was concomitant with a 15% decreased expression of Hsp72 in monocytes and granulocytes in both groups. This study provides evidence that in elderly, increased concentration of antioxidant vitamins C and E is associated with a reduction in oxidative stress and leukocytes Hsp72. In this context, 8 weeks of aerobic training has no impact on oxidative stress or leukocytes Hsp72 expression in elderly people.
L2: Attaining old age in good health is one of the major priorities of our society. The ability to cope with physiologically stressful conditions is known to play an important role in ‘successful aging’. Oxidative stress seems to play a pivotal role in the aging process and/or in the development of various diseases (1, 2). An increase in antioxidant capacity, mainly through an increase in the activity of antioxidant enzymes is usually observed in response to reactive oxygen species (ROS) (3). In addition to the upregulation of the antioxidant system in response to ROS overproduction, living organisms are able to synthesize heat shock proteins (HSP), a class of cytoprotective proteins involved in normal folding of newly synthesized polypeptides, or in the repair or removal of damaged proteins. These two adaptative responses to oxidative stress (increased antioxidant capacity and HSP expression) are essential to ensure cell survival and normal function of the main physiological systems (4). It has been clearly established that increased antioxidant capacity or over expression of the inducible form of the 70kDa family, i.e. Hsp72, provides greater protection for cellular and physiological function (5., 6., 7.). However, most studies focusing on the effects of aging on oxidative stress and HSP, particularly Hsp72, have reported that cells from old mammalians show higher levels of oxidative damage, lower antioxidant capacity and a reduced expression of Hsp72 (3, 8, 9). Interestingly, an increase in oxidative stress and a decline in Hsp72 induction in response to stress during aging has been linked with the pathogenesis or the worsening of various diseases (4, 10). Interventions that can preserve or restore the ability to synthesize Hsp72 or to limit oxidative stress have the potential to protect cells under stressful conditions and help in maintaining physiological function (i.e. cardiovascular, muscular or immunity) during aging.
L3: Physical activity and antioxidant supplementations are at the forefront of interventions that can potentially lower oxidative stress and increase Hsp72 expression. Antioxidant supplementation has been widely used to promote antioxidant capacity and limit oxidative damage during aging (11). In septuagenarians, 16 weeks of vitamin E supplementation successfully reduced lipid hydroperoxide levels (12). One year of a combination of high dose of vitamin C and vitamin E successfully reduced lipoperoxide levels in elderly people and was suggested to have some beneficial impact on bone health in this population (13). Hsp72 expression can also be altered by modifying the antioxidant capacity since the redox status of the cell is an important contributor to the synthesis of Hsp72 (14). However, conflicting results have been reported on the effect of antioxidant supplementation on Hsp72 expression. In young adults, an antioxidant mixture for 5 weeks did not change resting levels of Hsp72 in lymphocytes but increased the level of Hsp72 expression in response to heat shock in the presence of ROS (15). In a different study, 8 weeks of Vitamin C supplementation did not modify Hsp72 level in lymphocytes and blunted Hsp72 synthesis in response to ROS exposure (14).
L4: Physical exercise has also been reported to increase antioxidant capacity and/or stress induced Hsp72 expression in various tissues (16., 17., 18.) and could possibly contribute to reduce oxidative damage accumulation (19). In elderly people, 16 weeks of aerobic training significantly decreased oxidative stress markers and decreased blood pressure supporting a protective role of exercise to prevent oxidative stress-related conditions (12). In addition to its impact on oxidative stress, endurance training has also been shown to regulate Hsp72 expression (16, 19). Although both aerobic exercise and antioxidant supplementation can limit oxidative damage, potentially with an additive effect (20), their impact on Hsp72 expression remains unclear.
L5: Thus, the aim of that study was to investigate the effect of 8 weeks of aerobic training on systemic levels of oxidative stress markers and Hsp72 expression in leukocytes during vitamin C and E supplementation in septuagenarians. We hypothesised that i) increasing circulating antioxidant will lower markers of oxidative stress and leukocytes Hsp72 expression and ii) aerobic training in the context of antioxidant supplementation will reinforce this response.
L6: Sixteen healthy septuagenarians volunteered to participate in our study. They were all supplemented with vitamin C and E for 8 weeks and were randomly assigned either to a antioxidant supplemented sedentary group (AS, n = 8, = 4 and = 4, mean age = 73.4±8.7 yr) or to a antioxidant supplemented and aerobically trained group (AT, n = 8, = 4 and = 4, mean age = 75.8±2.8 yr). Prior to the study, the subjects were not engaged in any sporting activities and their physical activity was evaluated by a questionnaire validated in the elderly which quantifies household, leisure and sports activities (21). This questionnaire provides a method to classify participants into categories of low medium or high level of physical activity with cut off scores of 9.4 and 16.5, both groups falling into the medium category. During the experiment the subjects were asked not to modify their regular physical activity, except for the aerobic training sessions for AT group. Every subject had a clinical examination by a physician who was aware of the objective of the study. Exclusion criteria were: abnormal electrocardiogram or cardiovascular, pulmonary, metabolic, joint or neurological disease, and other pathologies that contraindicate maximal incremental testing (22). The subjects did not take any medication or vitamin or mineral supplements for at least 6 months before the study. The study design and purpose were fully explained to the subjects who all provided written consent to participate in this experimentation. The protocol was approved by the Human Ethics Committee from the Hospital/University of Montpellier (Ref: 02 02 01).
L7: During the first visit to the laboratory, each subject had a clinical examination. Then, they underwent a session of treadmill (TR) habituation and their preferred walking speed (PWS) was determined (23). One week later, the subjects came back to the laboratory to perform the initial maximal exercise test before the 8-week intervention and were asked not to participate in vigorous physical activity in the 48h prior to this visit. A fasting blood sample was collected at rest from an antecubital vein and then the subjects performed the warm-up and the graded exercise test (GXT) on TR, the total exercise session lasting at least 25 minutes. A post-exercise venous blood sample was drawn after the GXT. At the end of the 8- week intervention, the subjects went back to the laboratory to perform a post intervention GXT and the same protocol was applied, with blood samples taken before and after exercise.
L8: All the subjects performed the GXT on a TR (LE 200 CE Jaegger, Hoechberg, Germany) at their PWS, determined according to the methodology previously described (23). Briefly, each subject performed a TR acclimation walking at 5 different speeds (0.67, 0.89, 1.11, 1.33, 1.56 m.s-1). Then, the subject began walking at the lowest speed which was increased until she/he subjectively identified the PWS. This speed was maintained for 1 min and slightly modified. The same procedure was repeated starting from the highest of the 5 speeds and slowing down until the speed was identified again. The PWS was considered as the mean of the two speeds identified by the subject.
L9: The pre-intervention GXT, performed at least 48h after TR acclimation, began with 2 x 5 min of warm up at the PWS without grade, then the workload was increased using grade increase from 1 to 1.5%.min-1 until criterions for maximality were reached. During the test, oxygen uptake (VO2), CO2 output and ventilation were analyzed breath-by-breath using an on-line system (Oxycon Pro Jaegger, Hoechberg, Germany) and cardiac activity was monitored continuously using a 12-lead electrocardiogram. Criterion for maximality were: the sensation of fatigue, a heart rate within 5 b.min-1 of age specific maximal heart rate and at least one of the following criteria: occurrence of a 15-s plateau in oxygen uptake concurrent with continuing increase in power output or respiratory exchange ratio greater than 1.0 as previously described (22). If these criterion were not observed the subject was excluded from the study. The ventilatory threshold (TVE) was determined as described in the literature using 2 different methods: the gas exchange threshold method and the ventilatory method as previously described (22). The physician supervising the test was blinded to the treatment allocation.
L10: During the 8-week intervention, both groups received one capsule containing 500mg of vitamin C and another one containing 100 mg of vitamin E daily (24). During the same period, the AT group also performed supervised 1h-walking sessions, 3 days a week on an indoor running track. Each session started with 10 min of light walking with different types of exercises as a warm up. Then, the subjects performed 40 to 48 min of brisk walking. The intensity during these sessions was individualised at the heart rate corresponding to the TVE, and was progressively increased during the 8 weeks (25). For the whole duration of the experiment the participants were asked not to change their level of habitual physical activity. Compliance with the training was assessed in the AT group and all participants performed at least 90% of the sessions. Compliance with the supplementation, was assessed by interviewing the participant and counting the remaining pills and reached 98%.
L11: An automated haematology analyser (Pentra 120 retic ABX Diagnostics, Montpellier, France) was used to determine total and differential white blood cells counts. Plasma thiobarbituric acid reactive substances (TBARS), as markers of lipid peroxidations were determined by spectrofluorometry (26). The plasma levels of protein oxidation, using the assessment of advanced oxidation protein products (AOPP), were determined by spectrophotometry (27). Plasma vitamin C and vitamin E concentrations were investigated using high performance liquid chromatography (28, 29). Plasma concentrations of cholesterol and triglycerides were measured using a routine enzymatic method (Boehringer Mannheim, Meyland, France) and used to normalize Vitamin E levels, as the ratio of Vitamin E to blood lipid levels tend to remain constant and provide a better indicator of Vitamin E sufficiency (30).
L12: Cell death was investigated in leukocytes with the Annexin V-FITC kit for flow cytometry (ADK-700, StressGen Biotechnologies Corporation, Victoria, Canada) according to the manufacturer instructions. Briefly, 106 cells obtained from 7-ml sample of fresh EDTA treated blood, were stained with Annexin V and propidium iodide in buffer provided by the manufacturer for 10 min on ice and analyzed on a EPICS-XL- MLC flow cytometer (Beckman-Coulter, Villepinte, France). Cells only positive for Annexin V were considered as undergoing apoptosis and cells positive for both Annexin V and propidium iodide were considered as necrotic (22).
L13: Intracellular Hsp72 expression was measured by flow cytometry as previously described (22). Leukocytes (106) obtained from 7-ml sample of fresh EDTA treated blood, were fixed using reagent A from the Fix and Perm kit and according to the manufacturer’s instructions (An der Grub, Vienna, Austria). After being washed twice, cells were then permeabilized with the reagent B and incubated with a solution containing either 1µg of the specific Hsp72 monoclonal antibody (SPA 810; StressGen Biotechnologies Corporation, Victoria, Canada) or of the isotype matched monoclonal antibody (MG101; Caltag Laboratories, Burlingame, United States) for 20 min on ice in the dark. The cells were then washed twice and incubated with the secondary FITC-conjugated sheep antimouse F(ab’)2 IgG (SAB-102; StressGen Biotechnologies Corporation, Victoria, Canada) for 15 min on ice in the dark. After washing these twice, the cells were analyzed on the flow cytometer. Differentiation between lymphocytes, monocytes and granulocytes populations was performed according to difference in granularity and size. Data were presented as the mean fluorescence intensity in positive cells (MFI, i.e. Hsp72 expression in Hsp72-positive cells) for each leukocyte population and were corrected for background florescence with the negative control.
L14: The significant probability level was set at p < 0.05 and all values are given as mean ± SD. Differences between the two groups concerning age, height, body mass and physical activity score were tested using a one way ANOVA. The effect of the intervention on weight, plasmatic concentrations of vitamin C and E, VO2max and VO2 TVE in the two groups was investigated using ANOVA for repeated measures. The effect of the maximal exercise tests and of the intervention on leukocytes counts, oxidation markers, percentage of dead cells and expression of Hsp72 in leukocytes was investigated in the two groups using a two way ANOVA for repeated measures. When significant effect was found, post-hoc analysis was performed using the Fisher-test. When the assumption of normality was violated differences between the two groups were investigated using a Mann Whitney U-test and the effect of exercise and supplementation was tested using the Wilcoxon test. Statistical analysis was performed with Statistica 6.1 software (Statsoft, France).
L15: Anthropometry and aerobic fitness
L16: No significant difference was observed between the 2 groups concerning age, height, body mass, score of physical activity, VO2max and VO2 TVE before the intervention. The aerobic training only increased VO2 TVE (p < 0.05, Table 1) with no significant impact on VO2max.Table 1Anthropometric data and aerobic fitnessAS (n = 8)AT (n = 8)Pre trainingPost trainingPre trainingPost trainingAge (yr)73.4±8.775.8±2.8Height (cm)166±7165±10Body mass (kg)72.1±12.070.7±9.7Physical activity score14.2±4.412.9±3.5VO2max (ml.kg−1.min−1)27.3±2.826.7±3.027.5±4.727.4±4.7VO2TVE (ml.kg−1.min−1)22.0±1.621.9±1.721.6±2.923.5±3.3#AS = supplemented sedentary group); AT = supplemented and aerobically trained group; VO2max = maximal oxygen uptake; VO2TVE = oxygen uptake at the ventilatory threshold; #= significant effect of intervention, p < 0.05.White blood cell counts
L17: A significant leukocytosis was observed in response to the 2 GXT pre- and post-intervention in the 2 groups. In addition, the intervention induced a significant decrease in leukocyte counts in the 2 groups with no significant difference between them at any time of sampling (Table 2). The percentage of apoptotic leukocytes significantly decreased in response to the initial GXT in both groups, but only in the AT group after the post- intervention GXT (Table 2). The percentage of necrotic leukocytes was unchanged by either the intervention or the performance of the GXT and there was no difference between the 2 groups at any time of sampling.Table 2Antioxidants and white blood cells countsAS (n = 8)AT (n = 8)Pre trainingPost trainingPre trainingPost trainingVitamin C (µg.mL−1)10.1±2.420.3±4.4#9.3±3.817.5±3.3#Vitamin E / TG + Chol3.8±0.45.1±0.5#4.0±0.44.9±0.6#Leukocytes (109.L−1)Rest6.5±0.76.0±0.5#6.3±0.35.3±0.2#Post-ex7.0±0.8*6.6±0.5*#6.8±0.3*5.8±0.3*#Apoptotic leukocytes (%)Rest5.0±0.54.6±0.55.1±0.66.6±1.1Post-ex3.5±0.5*4.5±0.63.2±0.4*4.5±0.6*Necrotic leukocytes (%)Rest2.3±0.32.0±0.22.4±0.32.2±0.2Post-ex2.4±0.31.8±0.32.0±0.32.2±0.3AS = supplemented sedentary group; AT = supplemented and aerobically trained group; TG = triglycerides; Chol = cholesterol;*= significant effect of maximal exercise test, p < 0.05; #= significant effect of intervention, p < 0.05.Oxidative stress markers
L18: A significant increase in plasma vitamin C and vitamin E / triglycerides + cholesterol was observed in the 2 groups, in response to the 8-week of intervention (Table 2). Before intervention, TBARS and AOPP concentration significantly increased in response to GXT in the 2 groups and this response was still observed at the end of the 8-week intervention as well (p < 0.05, Figure 1). However, the intervention significantly reduced TBARS and AOPP levels at rest and in response to exercise in both groups (p < 0.05, Figure 1). No significant difference was observed for oxidant or antioxidant markers between the two groups at any time.Figure 1Oxidative stress markers TBARS = thiobarbituric acid reactive substances; AOPP: advanced oxidation protein products; *= significant effect of maximal exercise test, p < 0.05; #= significant effect of training/supplementation, p < 0.05.
L19: There was no difference in terms of Hsp72 expression between the 2 groups both at rest and in response to the GXT. The GXT itself did not modify Hsp72 expression but the intervention resulted in a significant decrease in Hsp72 expression from positive monocytes and granulocytes pre and post GXT in both groups (Figure 2). Neither the exercise nor the supplementation induced any significant adaptations in lymphocytes (Figure 2). No significant difference was observed at any time point and in any leukocytes sub populations between the two groups.Figure 2Intracellular Hsp72 in leukocytes MFI = mean fluorescence intensity in positive leukocytes; #= significant effect of training/supplementation, p < 0.05.
L20: No significant effect of gender could be observed on oxidative stress markers or antioxidant levels as well as on Hsp72 expression levels (p > 0.05; data not reported).Discussion
L21: This study aimed to investigate the effect of aerobic exercise in the context of antioxidant supplementation on systemic oxidative stress and leukocytes Hsp72 expression in elderly people. We provide evidence that increasing plasma vitamin C and E through an 8-week antioxidant supplementation results in a significant decrease in the levels of oxidative stress and in a reduced expression of Hsp72 in monocytes and granulocytes. In this context aerobic training did not bring any additional adaptations.
L22: In the present study, aerobic training induced a significant increase of sub-maximal aerobic parameters (i.e. VO2 TVE), indicating an increase in aerobic capacity, but did not modify VO2max This result is not surprising since the training sessions were conducted at an intensity corresponding to the TVE and were more effective in improving sub-maximal aerobic parameters rather than VO2max (31). Moreover, our purpose was to increase physical activity and aerobic capacity rather than VO2max since VO2 TVE is more representative of the capacity to perform everyday tasks in the elderly (32). It has been previously reported that 8 weeks of aerobic training only resulted in low increase in VO2max in older adults (33), whereas similar training for longer duration resulted in greater improvements in aerobic fitness (25, 31). The relatively short duration (8 weeks) of our training could be partly responsible for the lack of significant improvements in aerobic fitness observed in the present study.
L23: Before and after intervention, the maximal exercise test resulted in increased oxidative stress in both groups. This is in agreement with previous studies reporting ROS generation and oxidative stress following this type of exercise in different populations (22, 34). The increased concentration of vitamin C and E observed in both groups was associated with a significant decrease of AOPP and TBARS values (about 15 and 20% decrease respectively) both at rest and post-exercise, with no additional effect of aerobic training. We have previously reported that a decrease in oxidative damage of a similar magnitude through antioxidant supplementation contributed to restore muscle function in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (35) suggesting that even moderate changes in the oxidant/antioxidant balance can participate in preventing or improving organ dysfunctions associated with aging.
L24: It has been reported that ROS are important modulators of the physiological state of cells and could influence programmed cell death (36). However, in our experiment we found no relationship between oxidative stress markers and apoptotic or necrotic leukocytes. We observed a significant decrease in apoptotic leukocytes both pre- and post-intervention in the two groups in response to the maximal incremental tests, probably due to the release of a new pool of white blood cells in the systemic circulation, as supported by the important leukocytosis post-exercise (16, 22). The lack of additive adaptations when aerobic training was associated with antioxidant supplementation might reflect the dominant effect of antioxidants. In response to physical activity, the main stimulus responsible for the increased antioxidant capacity is known to be the increased production of ROS and the alteration of cellular redox status (3). It can be hypothesized that the increased antioxidant vitamin C and E reduced the accumulation of oxidative damage. This, in turn limited the alteration of the redox status, blunting the stimulation of the enzymatic antioxidant system (17). We can also postulate that the training intensity was not high enough to induce a sufficient imbalance in the cellular redox status. However, in our population VO2 TVE represented 80% of the participants’ maximal capacity. Thus, increasing any further the intensity of training could result in loss of adherence and increased risk of complications and should not be recommended in this population as previously described (37).
L25: In the present study, increasing plasma vitamin C and E was associated with a significant down regulation of Hsp72 in monocytes and granulocytes both at rest and in response to exercise. However, the association of aerobic training to antioxidant supplementation did not provide any further adaptations. In young adults, antioxidant supplementation with Vitamin C (14) or Vitamin E (38) have been shown to decrease leukocytes Hsp72 expression at rest, in response to exercise or after direct exposure to ROS. Acute exercise can exert its stimulating effect on Hsp72 through ROS production and alteration of the redox status of the cell (17). Antioxidant supplementation could then limit the accumulation of oxidized proteins and thus reduce the synthesis of Hsp72. This is consistent with our previous study showing that physically active elderly people characterised by a higher antioxidant capacity also showed lower levels of Hsp72 (19). Physical activity can also result in the induction of Hsp72 through an increase in temperature (39). In the present study, all training sessions were performed on an indoor running track. Although, it was not tightly regulated, ambient temperature was kept between 20° and 25°C. During exercise, core temperature is increased. However it has been shown that ambient temperature of greater than 28°C was required to significantly increase Hps72 expression in leukocytes. This could then explain the absence of effect of the aerobic training on Hsp72 expression (39).
L26: Consistent with previous reports, we observed higher levels of Hsp72 expression in monocytes and granulocytes when compared to lymphocytes (16, 19). As phagocytes, monocytes and granulocytes continuously produce and get exposed to ROS during inflammation or upon activation (16). This could explain the specific effect of the antioxidant vitamins on Hsp72 expression in those 2 subpopulations of leukocytes and the lack of response of lymphocytes. In the present study we did not observe any gender effect on either oxidative stress markers or Hsp72 expression as previously reported (22). Though, it has been shown that women could benefit from a better protection against oxidative stress than men, it seems that this protective effect is restricted to pre-menopausal age (40). An increased antioxidant capacity could impact on the cellular redox status and Hsp72 expression level. But, as only septuagenarians were recruited here, this probably prevented any effect of gender on the markers measured in this study.
L27: The absence of a placebo group could impact on the interpretation of the data. However, similar antioxidant supplementation have previously shown a significant impact on circulating levels of Vitamin C and E or antioxidant capacity, supporting the validity of the present results (14, 15, 24). It must also be acknowledged that the addition of an aerobically trained group only could have provided some useful information of the role of physical activity in the regulation of oxidative stress and Hsp72 during aging. Dietary intake was not controlled in this study and it cannot be completely ruled out that it could have an impact of the present results. However, vitamin levels in our participants were considered to be normal for their age group and did not indicate any deficiency (19). Although daily energy expenditure was not assessed in the present study, we used a questionnaire of physical activity that was specifically designed for elderly people. This allowed to quantify levels of habitual, leisure and sports physical activity (21) and which has shown a fair-to-moderate validity in measuring energy expenditure (41). It has been reported a lack of specificity of TBARS as oxidative stress marker, but changes in response to the exercise test or the intervention were consistent with those observed on AOPP (marker of proteins oxidation), and both markers have been widely used in clinical practice (22).
L28: In conclusion, this study shows that in elderly people, increasing plasma vitamin C and E contributes to lower oxidative stress and Hsp72 expression in leukocytes. However, aerobic training in the context of antioxidant supplementation does not impact on those biological markers thought to be critical in the aging process.
L29: AcknowledgementsWe are indebted to all the subjects who participated in this study. We are very grateful to Professors Christian Prefaut, Jean Pierre Vendrell and Jean Paul Cristol, Doctors Anne Marie Dupuy, Helmi Ben Saad, Sami Bouaziz and Jean Michel Fondere, as well as Nathalie Mialanes and Stephanie Badiou for technical support. We also want to thank Pierrick Bernard and Ludovic Marin for their precious help during the training program.
